1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,546
Every cell in your body
carries the same DNA.

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,785
So why does a neuron behave
nothing like the cell beside it?

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,552
In the brain, each cell type
reads different genes.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,213
Grind the tissue together, and
those signals are diluted.

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:22,340
Inside every nucleus, DNA is wrapped
around proteins called histones.

6
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,407
Small marks on histone tails
decide which genes are read.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,292
H3K27ac opens the DNA, so
the gene can be read.

8
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,929
H3K27me3 packs it tightly, and
the gene stays silent.

9
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,867
To measure these marks,
NEXUS built HisTLAND®.

10
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,194
It reads two histone marks from one
sample, in bulk or in single cells.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,929
Epigenetic profiling has another layer, DNA
methylation, written on the DNA itself.

12
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,561
For that layer, NEXUS built MeTLAND®.

13
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,796
It maps DNA methylation without
bisulfite or antibodies.

14
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:12,388
NEXUS Epigenomics, founded in Stockholm, builds kits
around published methods from academic labs.

15
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,146
Ready to run in your own lab.

16
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,566
Two layers of the epigenome.
HisTLAND® and MeTLAND®.

17
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:29,406
What is the question you are
solving in epigenetics?
